    bestQuotes: [
      { text: 'You can choose to control your focus, or you can let someone else take it away from you.', cite: 'Thibaut Meurisse' },
      { text: 'Excitement and fulfillment aren\'t the same things.', cite: 'Thibaut Meurisse' },
      { text: 'The truth is that no amount of stimulation will ever bring you the sense of fulfillment you\'re seeking.', cite: 'Thibaut Meurisse' },
      { text: 'Dopamine is not a pleasure chemical. It is the force that makes us act.', cite: 'Thibaut Meurisse' },
      { text: 'Your focus is worth a lot of money — which is why YouTube, Facebook, and Instagram do everything they can to keep you glued to your screen.', cite: 'Thibaut Meurisse' },
    ],
    finalTakeaway: 'Dopamine Detox is a short, no-nonsense productivity primer by Thibaut Meurisse, the first book in his Productivity Series, written for anyone who procrastinates on important work and feels restless and unfocused. Its core argument is simple: dopamine is not a pleasure chemical but the "molecule of more" — a neurotransmitter that drives us to seek rewards — and in a world engineered to hijack it, our constant overstimulation makes hard, important tasks feel unbearable. The solution is a dopamine detox: deliberately reducing stimulation so you return to a more natural baseline, where challenging and boring work becomes genuinely more appealing. The book is practical rather than preachy. It explains three types of detox (the demanding 48-hour complete detox, the 24-hour detox, and the gentler partial detox) and gives a clear three-step method: identify your biggest distractions, add friction to make bad behaviors harder and good ones easier, and plan your day with 3–5 key tasks in order of importance. It then addresses the harder parts of the process — doing the work by starting with the needle-moving task you most want to avoid, and avoiding "dopamine relapse" by staying self-aware, accepting slips without guilt, and recommitting one day at a time. The book\'s underlying message is empowering: excitement and fulfillment are not the same, your focus is worth real money to the platforms that fight for it — and worth even more to you. By choosing to control your focus instead of letting others take it, you can reclaim the ability to do hard things, become far more productive, and live a calmer, more fulfilling life.'
